{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"bloated-shadeful-lamprey","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/_lib_writes.py` measures **778 lines** against the hard 800-line module-size gate\n(`.github/module-size-limit.txt`), which CI enforces with no allowlist. The sibling story that adds\ntwo optional create arguments has to touch two `@overload` signatures plus the implementation and\ntheir docstrings, which will not fit in 22 lines.\n\n## Approach\n\nExtract lines **483-724** into a new `_lib_mutations.py`, anchored on the `_python_leaf` call graph.\n`_python_leaf` (`:483`) is called by `comment` (`:527`), `edit_ticket` (`:620`), `link` (`:649`),\n`unlink` (`:655`), `tag` (`:661`), `untag` (`:667`) and `archive` (`:673`) — seven callers, all\ninside the block, and nothing above line 483 calls it.\n\nStated honestly: `append_session_log`, `start_session_log`, `compact` and `attach_commits` ride along\nbecause they are the contiguous remainder of the post-genesis mutation surface, not because they call\n`_python_leaf`. The strict call-graph-only subset is 122 lines and non-contiguous, which would force a\nscattered cut and a module close to the 100-line floor. Taking the contiguous mutation block keeps one\ncoherent unit at ~264 lines.\n\nTwo clusters were considered and rejected. The identity cluster (`:182-327`, 146 lines) is a\n*shared-dependency* group — all seven functions delegate to `rebar._commands.identity` but none calls\nanother — so extracting it would be the mechanical split the module-size policy forbids. The signing\ncluster (`:727-778`) is 52 lines, below the 100-line floor.\n\nConsumers are re-pointed rather than re-exported where that is cheap: `rebar/_lib_gates.py:18`\nimports `_python_leaf` from `_lib_writes`, and `rebar/__init__.py:86-115` re-exports eleven of the\nmoved names. `_lib_gates.py` is itself the sibling precedent for this split. There are **zero**\n`getattr` / `monkeypatch.setattr` / `importlib` string references to `_lib_writes` attributes anywhere\nin the tree, so the reference set is fully visible to static tooling.\n\nThis is a behaviour-preserving move: no logic is rewritten.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_lib_mutations.py` (new) — the moved block, ~264 lines\n- `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py` — drops to ~544 lines\n- `src/rebar/_lib_gates.py` — `_python_leaf` import re-pointed\n- `src/rebar/__init__.py` — the public re-export block re-pointed\n\n## Testing\n\nNo new behaviour, so the oracle is the existing suite passing unchanged. The specific risk here is the\n**public API surface**, since eleven names are re-exported as `rebar.<name>`:\n\n- Public-surface assertion: every name previously importable as `rebar.<name>` — `comment`, `link`,\n  `unlink`, `tag`, `untag`, `archive`, `compact`, `attach_commits`, `edit_ticket`,\n  `append_session_log`, `start_session_log` — still resolves after the move, asserted by name rather\n  than assumed from a passing suite.\n- `rebar._python_leaf` still resolves, since `__init__.py:87` re-exports it and `_lib_gates` depends\n  on it at three call sites.\n- Import-cycle check: `_lib_writes` and `_lib_mutations` import cleanly in either order.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `_lib_writes.py` and `_lib_mutations.py` are each at or below 650 lines; checked by the\n      module-size gate with both counts recorded.\n- [ ] Every one of the eleven previously public `rebar.<name>` mutation entry points still resolves;\n      checked by a test importing each by name.\n- [ ] `rebar._python_leaf` still resolves and `_lib_gates` uses it at its three call sites; checked by\n      importing it and running the gates tests.\n- [ ] The full existing test suite passes with no test-file edits; checked by the gating test command\n      and confirming no file under `tests/` changed.\n- [ ] The two modules import cleanly in either order; checked by an explicit both-orders import test.","id":"4631-5598-7127-4a56","parent_id":"0e68-41eb-5782-4336","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Size relief: extract the _python_leaf mutation block from _lib_writes.py into _lib_mutations.py"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786162196192320001,"uuid":"4fecc497-6344-4f0b-9ccd-14b9ec250689"}